[[File:Bougainvillea, Behbahan.jpg|alt=Bougainvillea, Behbahan|thumb|Bougainvillea, [[Behbahan]]. Many of the small white flowers, in various stages of development, may be seen among the larger bracts.]]

'''''Bougainvillea''''' ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|b|uː|ɡ|ən|ˈ|v|ɪ|l|i|.|ə}} {{respell|BOO|gən|VIL|ee|ə}}, {{IPAc-en|USalso|ˌ|b|oʊ|-}} {{respell|BOH|-}}) is a genus of thorny ornamental vines, bushes, and trees belonging to the family [[Nyctaginaceae]]. They are native to the tropical forests of [[South America]] (specifically [[Brazil]], [[Bolivia]], [[Paraguay]], [[Peru]], [[Uruguay]] and [[Argentina]]). There are between 4 and 22 species in the genus.<ref name="WFO"/> The [[inflorescence]] consists of large colorful [[sepal|sepal-like]] [[bract]]s which surround three simple waxy flowers, gaining popularity for the plant as an ornamental. The plant is named after explorer [[Louis Antoine de Bougainville]] (1729–1811), after it was documented on one of his expeditions.

==Description==
[[File:Starr 080610-8171 Bougainvillea spectabilis.jpg|thumb|left|Close-up photo of ''[[Bougainvillea spectabilis]]'' in Hawaii, showing thorns]]
The species grows {{convert|1|to|12|m|ft|0}} tall, scrambling over other plants with their spiky thorns. They are [[evergreen]] where rainfall occurs all year, or [[deciduous]] if there is a [[dry season]]. The [[leaf|leaves]] are alternate, simple ovate-acuminate, {{convert|4-13|cm||abbr=on}} long and {{convert|2-6|cm||abbr=on|sigfig=2}} broad. The actual [[flower]] of the plant is small and generally white, but each cluster of three flowers is surrounded by three or six [[bract]]s with the bright colours associated with the plant, including pink, magenta, purple, red, orange, white, or yellow. ''[[Bougainvillea glabra]]'' is sometimes called "paper flower" because its bracts are thin and papery. The [[fruit]] is a narrow five-lobed [[achene]].

==History==
The first European to describe these plants was [[Philibert Commerçon]], a [[botanist]] accompanying French Navy admiral [[Louis Antoine de Bougainville]] during his voyage of circumnavigation of the [[Earth]], and first published by [[Antoine Laurent de Jussieu]] in 1789.<ref>{{cite web|title=Bougainvillea Comm. ex Juss.|url=http://www.tropicos.info/Name/40030049?projectid=30|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131016014921/http://www.tropicos.info/Name/40030049?projectid=30|url-status=dead|archive-date=October 16, 2013|work=Tropicos|publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden}}</ref>
[[File:Bougainvillea glabra at Kadavoor.jpg|thumb|right|''[[Bougainvillea glabra]]'' in [[Kerala]]]] 
Twenty years after Commerçon's description, it was first published as 'Buginvillæa' in [[Genera Plantarum]] by [[Antoine Laurent de Jussieu|A. L. de Jussieu]] in 1789.<ref>[https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/item/7125#page/186/mode/1up Jussieu, A.L. de. Genera Plantarum]</ref> The genus was subsequently spelled in several ways until it was finally established as "''Bougainvillea''" in the [[Index Kewensis]] in the 1930s. Originally, ''B.&nbsp;spectabilis'' and ''B.&nbsp;glabra'' were undifferentiated until the mid-1980s when botanists classified them as distinct species. In the early 19th century, these two species were the first to be introduced into Europe, and soon nurseries in France and Britain sold these varieties in Australia and throughout their former colonies. Meanwhile, [[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew|Kew Gardens]] distributed plants it had propagated to British colonies throughout the world. Soon thereafter, a crimson specimen in Cartagena, Colombia was added to the genus descriptions. Originally thought to be a distinct species, it was named ''B.&nbsp;buttiana'' in honor of the European who first encountered it. However, later studies classified it as a natural hybrid of a variety of ''B.&nbsp;glabra'' and possibly ''B.&nbsp;peruviana''—a "local pink bougainvillea" from Peru. Natural hybrids were soon found to be common occurrences all over the world. For instance, around the 1930s, when the three species were grown together, many hybrid crosses were produced almost spontaneously in East Africa, India, the Canary Islands, Australia, North America, and the Philippines.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}

==Cultivation and uses==
[[File:Pink Bougainvillea.jpg|thumb|left|A "stick" of pink bougainvillea]]
[[File:Buganvílea na frente do Colégio São Benedito, Bagé-RS.jpg|thumb|''Bougainvillea sp.'' in Brazil]]
Bougainvillea are popular [[ornamental plant]]s in most areas with warm climates, including [[Florida]], [[South Carolina]],<ref>{{Cite book|title=The Concord Desk Encyclopedia|last=TIME|publisher=Concord Reference Books|year=1982|isbn=0-940994-01-1|pages=185}}</ref> [[South India]], [[California]], and across the [[Mediterranean Basin]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Manzaneque |first=Fernando |date=2023-09-14 |title=Bougainvillea, A "Mediterranean" Staple |url=https://medium.com/weeds-wildflowers/bougainvillea-a-mediterranean-staple-2edc974e0345 |access-date=2024-01-08 |website=Weeds & Wildflowers |language=en}}</ref>

Although it is frost-sensitive and hardy in USDA [[hardiness zone|Hardiness Zones]] 9b and 10, bougainvillea can be used as a houseplant or hanging basket in cooler climates. In the landscape, it makes an excellent hot season plant, and its drought tolerance makes it ideal for warm climates year-round. Its high salt tolerance makes it a natural choice for color in coastal regions. It can be pruned into a standard, but is also grown along fence lines, on walls, in containers and hanging baskets, and as a hedge or an accent plant. Its long arching thorny branches bear heart-shaped leaves and masses of papery bracts in white, pink, orange, purple, and burgundy. Many cultivars, including double-flowered and variegated, are available.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}

Many bougainvillea today are the result of interbreeding among only three out of the eighteen South American species recognized by botanists. There are over 300 varieties of bougainvillea. Because many of the hybrids have been crossed over several generations, it is difficult to identify their respective origins. Natural mutations seem to occur spontaneously throughout the world; wherever large numbers of plants are being produced, [[Sport (botany)|bud-sports]] will occur. This had led to multiple names for the same cultivar (or variety) and has added to the confusion over the names of bougainvillea cultivars.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}

The growth rate of bougainvillea varies from slow to rapid, depending on the variety. They tend to flower all year round in equatorial regions. Elsewhere, they are seasonal, with bloom cycles typically four to six weeks. Bougainvillea grow best in dry soil, in very bright full sun and with frequent fertilisation; but they require little water once established, and in fact will not flourish if over-watered. They can be easily propagated via tip cuttings.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Martin Anderson, Texas AgriLife Extension Service |title=Bougainvillea {{!}} Archives {{!}} Aggie Horticulture |url=https://aggie-horticulture.tamu.edu/archives/parsons/publications/bougainvillea.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220522043705/https://aggie-horticulture.tamu.edu/archives/parsons/publications/bougainvillea.html |archive-date=2022-05-22 |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=aggie-horticulture.tamu.edu |language=en}}</ref>

''Bougainvillea'' is also a very attractive genus for Bonsai enthusiasts, due to their ease of training and their radiant flowering during the spring.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ichiban |first=Mark- |date=2020-01-02 |title=Bougainvillea Bonsai Tree - Species Guide and Growing Tips |url=https://www.bonsaidojo.net/bougainvillea-bonsai-species-guide/ |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=BonsaiDojo |language=en-US}}</ref> They can be kept as indoor [[houseplant]]s in temperate regions and kept small by [[bonsai]] techniques.

[[Bougainvillea × buttiana|''B.'' × ''buttiana'']] is a garden hybrid of [[Bougainvillea glabra|''B. glabra'']] and [[Bougainvillea peruviana|''B. peruviana'']]. It has produced numerous garden-worthy cultivars.

The cultivars 'San Diego Red'<ref>{{Cite web |title=Bougainvillea 'San Diego Red' {{!}} bougainvillea 'San Diego Red' Climber Wall Shrub/RHS |url=http://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/99916/bougainvillea-san-diego-red/details |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=www.rhs.org.uk |language=en-gb}}</ref> and 'Mary Palmer's Enchantment' <ref>{{Cite web |title=Bougainvillea (Spectoperuviana Group) 'Mary Palmer's Enchantment' {{!}} bougainvillea 'Mary Palmer's Enchantment' Climber Wall Shrub/RHS |url=http://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/153118/bougainvillea-(spectoperuviana-group)-mary-palmer-s-enchantment/details |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=www.rhs.org.uk |language=en-gb}}</ref> have gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]]. 
''Bougainvillea'' are relatively pest-free plants, but they may be susceptible to worms, snails and [[aphid]]s. The [[larva]]e of some [[Lepidoptera]] species also use them as food plants, for example the [[giant leopard moth]] (''Hypercompe scribonia'').
[[File:Island_Greece_Santorini_Flowers_Blue_Travel_Sea.jpg|thumb|Its low water requirements make the bougainvillea an ideal plant for the decoration of dry places, such as the [[List of islands of Greece|Greek island]] of [[Santorini]] depicted.]]

==Symbolism and nomenclature==
[[File:Никитский ботанический сад 022.jpg|thumb|Bougainvillea in [[Southern Coast (Crimea)|southern Crimea]], [[Ukraine]]. The [[humid subtropical climate]] in the area allows the cultivation of certain cultivars.]]
Various species of ''Bougainvillea'' are the official flowers of [[Guam]] (where it is known as the ''Puti Tai Nobiu'');<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ctahr.hawaii.edu/oc/freepubs/pdf/OF-38.pdf |first=Kent D. |last=Kobayashi |author2=James McConnell |author3=John Griffis |title=Bougainvillea |date=October 2007 |publisher=Cooperative Extension Service, University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa}}</ref> [[Matsu Islands|Lienchiang]] and [[Pingtung County|Pingtung Counties]] in [[Taiwan]]; [[Ipoh]], [[Malaysia]];<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.perak.info/kinta/ipoh.htm |title=Welcome to Ipoh – The Bougainvillea City |publisher=Passage to Kinta District |access-date=2010-12-14 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110721231201/http://www.perak.info/kinta/ipoh.htm |archive-date=2011-07-21 }}</ref> the cities of [[Tagbilaran]], [[Philippines]]; [[Camarillo, California]]; [[Laguna Niguel, California]]; [[San Clemente, California]]; the cities of [[Shenzhen]], [[Huizhou]], [[Zhuhai]], and [[Jiangmen]] in [[Guangdong]] Province, [[China]]; [[Xiamen, Fujian]]<ref>{{cite news |title=Xiamen City Flower and City Tree |url=http://www.cdsndu.org/html_en/to_specialArticleContent_specialArticleID=8a28e6d84afcab3e014b01554ff103de.html |access-date=2021-03-16 |work=www.cdsndu.org |archive-date=2021-03-16 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210316131820/http://www.cdsndu.org/html_en/to_specialArticleContent_specialArticleID=8a28e6d84afcab3e014b01554ff103de.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> and [[Naha, Okinawa]]. It is also the national flower of [[Grenada]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Webmaster |first=G. O. G. |date=2022-12-06 |title=National Symbols |url=https://www.gov.gd/index.php/national-symbols |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=Government of Grenada {{!}} Web Portal |language=en-gb}}</ref>

Native to South America, bougainvillea carry several names in the different regions where they are present. Apart from [[Rioplatense Spanish]] ''santa-rita'', [[Colombian Spanish]] ''veranera'', [[Peruvian Spanish]] ''papelillo'', [[Caribbean Spanish]] ''trinitaria'', it may be variously named ''primavera'', ''três-marias'', ''sempre-lustrosa'', ''santa-rita'', ''ceboleiro'', ''roseiro'', ''roseta'', ''riso'', ''pataguinha'', ''pau-de-roseira'' and ''flor-de-papel'' in [[Brazilian Portuguese]]. Nevertheless, {{lang|pt|buganvília}} {{IPA|pt|buɡɐ̃ˈviʎ̟ɐ|}} in Portuguese and {{lang|es|buganvilia}} {{IPA|es|buɣamˈbilja|}} in Spanish are the most common names accepted by people of the regions where these languages are spoken but it is an introduced plant.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024}}

==Toxicity==
The sap of bougainvillea can cause serious [[contact dermatitis|skin rashes]], similar to ''[[Toxicodendron]]'' species.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Phytodermatitis: Reactions in the Skin Caused by Plants |url=http://www.lni.wa.gov/safety/research/dermatitis/files/phytoderm.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141127084340/http://www.lni.wa.gov/safety/research/dermatitis/files/phytoderm.pdf |archive-date=2014-11-27 |access-date=2026-02-13 |website=www.lni.wa.gov}}</ref>

